  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Direct Answer: While metformin remains the traditional first-line therapy for type 2 diabetes, major organizations like the European Association for the Study of Diabetes (EASD) and the American Diabetes Association (ADA) now recommend considering SGLT2 inhibitors (SGLT2i) or GLP-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RA) as first-line options for certain high-risk patients, individualized based on cardiorenal profile, weight, and comorbidities rather than a rigid glucose-only algorithm."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Efficacy and Glycemic Control"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "GLP-1 RAs: Reduce HbA1c more potently, typically by 0.7% to 1.7% ."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "SGLT2 Inhibitors: Reduce HbA1c by 0.3% to 1.2% (insulin-independent urinary glucose excretion)."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Hypoglycemia Risk: Both classes carry a low inherent risk of low blood sugar unless combined with insulin or sulfonylureas."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          1,
          2,
          3
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Weight Loss and Blood Pressure"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "GLP-1 RAs: Cause modest, dose-dependent weight loss and small systolic blood pressure reductions (2.8\u20135.6 mmHg), though often accompanied by a slight increase in heart rate (3\u20134 bpm)."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "SGLT2 Inhibitors: Cause consistent weight loss of 1.5\u20133 kg and modest blood pressure reductions without raising heart rate."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Cardiovascular and Renal Protection"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "SGLT2 Inhibitors: Show superior benefits for heart failure hospitalization reduction (in both HFrEF and HFpEF) and chronic kidney disease (CKD) progression prevention (e.g., via Diabetes Care ).",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "Diabetes Care",
                "link": "https://diabetesjournals.org/care/article/41/8/1543/36374/SGLT2-Inhibitors-in-Combination-Therapy-From"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "GLP-1 RAs: Show superior benefits for atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD), coronary artery disease, and ischemic stroke prevention (e.g., semaglutide or dulaglutide) .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Major Adverse Cardiovascular Events (MACE): Both classes offer robust MACE reduction compared to older agents like DPP-4 inhibitors or sulfonylureas, with comparable head-to-head MACE risk."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          7,
          8
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Side Effects and Contraindications"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "SGLT2 Inhibitors:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Side effects : Genital mycotic/fungal infections, urinary tract infections, volume depletion/hypotension."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Rare risks: Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": ": Generally avoided or discontinued if eGFR drops below 30 mL/min/1.73 m\u00b2 (though renal protective benefits persist if started earlier)."
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2,
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "GLP-1 RAs:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Side effects : Gastrointestinal upset (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea), requiring slow dose titration."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Contraindications/Cautions: History of gastroparesis, active gallbladder disease, personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 2 (MEN2), and caution with proliferative retinopathy."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          2,
          9,
          10,
          11
        ]
      },
      {
